\section{Run and edit the app locally}{

Use \code{app = "local"} to run Shiny with your installed R packages while
Docker runs the database and sign-in service. Install callr and bslib
0.9.0 or later, plus the source's R client and database driver. PostgREST
works without a database driver and is a useful first option.

Trino needs RTrino. StarRocks needs odbc and MySQL Connector/ODBC with its
OpenID Connect plugin. DuckDB needs a matching client (1.5.4 for the demo)
and its Quack extensions. Native PostgreSQL needs Linux with RPostgres
and shinyOAuthDB linked to the same libpq 18.

Use \code{action = "files"} to copy the app, then edit
\verb{local-app/<database>/app.R}. Start with the same \code{path}, save your edits
and reload the browser. Existing files are preserved; use a new empty
path for fresh templates.

Local mode needs Compose 2.24.4 or later. It trusts the demo certificate
only in the app process, leaving your certificate store unchanged.
StarRocks needs about 4 GB of Docker memory and amd64 support; OpenSearch
needs about 2 GB for its database container.
}

\section{Run your own app with Docker services}{

Use \code{action = "services"} to start the database and sign-in service,
then return to R without starting Shiny. This needs Compose 2.24.4 or later.
Pass the returned \code{provider} to \code{shinyOAuth::oauth_client()} and \code{url} to
the data helper. The PostgREST example works without a database driver;
native clients still need their usual drivers.

Keep the returned handle, such as \code{demo}, while your app runs. Register
\code{demo$stop} with \code{shiny::onStop()} or call \code{demo$stop()} when finished.
It removes containers and sample data and restores local-development
options. Reuse the handle until you stop it.

Browser sign-in uses HTTPS. Services-only PostgREST uses HTTP on
\verb{127.0.0.1:19444}, allowed only while the demo is active. Other endpoints
use HTTPS; the handle's \code{ca_bundle} supplies their local certificate.
}

